.TH OTPME-SCOPE 1 "2026-10-10" "OTPme 0.3.0a281" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-scope \- say what an application may learn about a user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-scope
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIscope\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A scope is what an application asks for when it sends a user to the SSO portal to sign in, and what OTPme hands out in return.
\fBemail\fR gets the application the mail address of the user, \fBprofile\fR their name, \fBgroups\fR the groups they are in.
The names come from OpenID Connect; OTPme is the OpenID Provider and the application is the Relying Party, see
.BR otpme (7).
.PP
The application is a
.B client
of the realm, and that is the word for the object as well \(em the one
.BR otpme\-client (1)
manages.
Where this page says application, that object is what holds it.
.PP
A scope object says who may hand that scope out.
It holds tokens and roles \(em the users that may give it away \(em and the clients that may receive it.
A scope is granted only if all three agree: the application asked for it, the token of the user has it, and the client has it.
See \fBHOW A SCOPE IS GRANTED\fR.
.PP
So this is the second of two questions about one login.
The access group of the client decides whether the user gets in at all (see \fBotpme\-accessgroup\fR(1)); the scopes decide what the application is told about them afterwards.
.PP
A scope lives in the \fBscopes\fR unit of a site, and the nodes of that site are what answers with it.
OIDC does not cross a site boundary: a scope is only ever looked up on the site that owns it, and the clients and groups it takes are the ones of that site.
.PP
Every site is created with seven scopes of its own, see \fBTHE SCOPES A SITE BRINGS\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Scope Management
.TP
.B add \fIscope\fR \fR[\fIscope_id\fR]
Create a scope.
.br
It is created in the \fBscopes\fR unit of your site; a path puts it into another unit, and
.B move
takes it there afterwards.
A name is unique in its site, whatever unit it sits in.
.br
\fIscope_id\fR is the name the application sends, and without one it is the name of the scope, see \fBTHE SCOPE ID\fR.
.br
A new scope holds nobody.
Both sides have to be named: the clients that receive it, with
.BR add_client ,
and the roles whose users hand it out, with
.BR add_role .
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:scope\fR on the unit it is created in, not on the scope \(em there is none yet.
.TP
.B del \fR[\fB\-\-no\-trash\fR] \fIscope\fR
Delete a scope.
It goes to the trash, from where
.BR otpme\-trash (1)
can bring it back; \fB\-\-no\-trash\fR deletes it right away.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdelete:object\fR on the scope, or \fBdelete:scope\fR on the unit it sits in.
.br
A scope a site brings with it cannot be deleted.
Switch it off with
.B disable
instead.
.TP
.B show \fR[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-z\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-fields\fR \fIF1,F2\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\-by\fR \fIfield\fR] [\fB\-\-reverse\fR] [\fB\-\-raw\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR \fIsep\fR] \fR[\fIscope\fR]
Without a scope: a table of all of them, with their unit, their roles, tokens, clients and groups, their scope ID and their policies.
.RS
.TP
.B \-a
Also the scopes you may only look at, not just the ones you may change.
.TP
.B \-z \fIN\fR
How many scopes to print.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIF1,F2\fR
Print these columns and no others.
The names are the ones in the header of the table.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-policy\-limit \fIN\fR
How many items to print per scope, and the same for the policies alone.
.TP
.B \-\-sort\-by \fIfield\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\fR
Which column to sort by, and the other way round.
.TP
.B \-\-raw\fR, \fB\-\-csv\fR, \fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR \fIsep\fR
For reading with something other than your eyes: \fB\-\-raw\fR leaves the header and the borders out, \fB\-\-csv\fR prints one row per line with \fB;\fR between the columns, and \fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR uses another separator.
.RE
.IP
A column you may not see says \fB\-\fR.
Which ACL opens which column is the \fBview:\fR ACL of that value, and the \fBedit:\fR, \fBenable:\fR or \fBremove:\fR ACL of it does as well \(em whoever may set a thing may read it.
.br
With a scope named, this prints the object itself instead of a table row: its scope ID and its roles, tokens, clients and groups, each line a value.
A reference to an object that is gone is printed as the bare UUID, which is how
.B remove_orphans
has something to find.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-\-attribute\fR \fIattribute\fR] \fR[\fIregex\fR]
List the scope names, and with a regex only those that match it.
You get the scopes you may change; \fB\-a\fR adds those you may only look at.
.br
\fB\-\-attribute\fR matches the regex against that attribute instead of the name: \fBlist \-\-attribute scope_id email\fR lists the scope an application asks for as \fBemail\fR.
.TP
.B enable \fIscope\fR
Enable a disabled scope.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBenable:object\fR.
.TP
.B disable \fIscope\fR
Switch a scope off without deleting it.
.br
It is then handed out to nobody, and it is gone from the \fIscopes_supported\fR list of the discovery document.
An application that asks for it still gets its login; the scope is simply missing from the answer.
.br
This is what to do with a scope a site brings with it, which cannot be deleted.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdisable:object\fR.
.TP
.B rename \fIscope\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename a scope.
.br
The name is not what the application sends \(em that is the scope ID, and a rename leaves it alone.
A scope a site brings with it cannot be renamed.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBrename:object\fR.
.TP
.B move \fR[\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR] \fIscope\fR \fIunit\fR
Move the scope into another unit.
Its ACLs move with it: the default ACLs of the old unit are taken away and those of the new one are applied.
\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR leaves the ACLs of the scope as they are.
.br
A scope does not inherit ACLs by default, see
.B enable_acl_inheritance
below.
.TP
.B scope_id \fIscope\fR \fIscope_id\fR
Set the name the application sends for this scope, see \fBTHE SCOPE ID\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBedit:scope_id\fR.
.TP
.B touch \fIscope\fR
Re-index the object to fix potential index problems.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBtouch\fR.
.SS Who May Hand the Scope Out
A token is named by its path, \fIuser\fB/\fItoken\fR.
A role is named by its name, which is looked for in the site of the scope, or as \fIsite\fB/\fIrole\fR to take one from another site of the realm.
.TP
.B add_token \fIscope\fR \fItoken_path\fR
Let this token hand the scope out.
.br
Whoever signs in with it can give the scope to an application that asks for it \(em if that application has the scope as well.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:token\fR.
.TP
.B remove_token \fIscope\fR \fItoken_path\fR
Take the token out of the scope.
A UUID is taken as well, which is how a reference to a token that is gone is cleared out.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:token\fR.
.TP
.B list_tokens \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-token\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fR[\fIscope\fR]
List the tokens in the scope, disabled ones included.
.br
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR selects what is printed per token; the default is the path.
\fB\-\-token\-types\fR lists only tokens of the given types, for example \fBhotp,totp\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:tokens\fR.
.TP
.B add_role \fIscope\fR \fIrole\fR
Let every token of this role hand the scope out.
.br
This is the way to do it for more than one user: a role holds the tokens, the scope holds the role.
A role from another site is named \fIsite\fB/\fIrole\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:role\fR.
.TP
.B remove_role \fIscope\fR \fIrole\fR
Take the role out of the scope.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:role\fR.
.TP
.B list_roles \fIscope\fR
List the roles in the scope.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:roles\fR.
.SS Which Applications Get It
A client is named by its name and has to be a client of the site of the scope, see
.BR otpme\-client (1).
.TP
.B add_client \fIscope\fR \fIclient\fR
Let this application receive the scope.
.br
One scope ID per application: adding a client that another scope with the same scope ID already holds is refused, and the other scope is named.
That is what keeps the answer unambiguous, see \fBTHE SCOPE ID\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:client\fR.
.TP
.B remove_client \fIscope\fR \fIclient\fR
Take the application out of the scope.
From then on it no longer gets it, whatever it asks for.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:client\fR.
.TP
.B list_clients \fIscope\fR
List the applications in the scope, disabled ones included.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:clients\fR.
.SS The Groups Claim
This list is read by one scope only, the one whose scope ID is \fBgroups\fR.
What it does is under \fBTHE GROUPS CLAIM\fR below.
.TP
.B add_group \fIscope\fR \fIgroup\fR
Allow this group to be named to the application.
.br
The group has to be a group of the site of the scope, see
.BR otpme\-group (1).
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:group\fR.
.TP
.B remove_group \fIscope\fR \fIgroup\fR
Take the group off the list.
The application is no longer told about it, even if the user is in it.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:group\fR.
.TP
.B list_groups \fIscope\fR
List the groups on the list.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:groups\fR.
.SS Object Changelog
Every change to the object writes an entry: who made it, when, and what was done.
A change can carry a comment of its own, through the global option \fB\-\-changelog\fR.
.PP
An \fIentry_id\fR is the number the listing shows in front of an entry, counted from 0.
.PP
Only the comment of an entry can be changed or removed.
What was done and by whom stays as it is.
.TP
.B changelog \fIscope\fR
Show the changelog of the scope.
Needs the ACL \fBview:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fIscope\fR \fIentry_id\fR \fIcomment\fR
Set the comment of that entry, or replace the one it has.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fIscope\fR \fIentry_id\fR
Remove the comment of that entry.
The entry itself stays, and an entry without a comment is refused.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fIscope\fR
Remove every entry of the changelog.
.br
This needs more than the ACL \fBclear:changelog\fR: the config parameter
.B allow_changelog_clearing
has to allow it (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
It is true by default, but where no value is found at all, clearing is refused.
OTPme asks the token you are signed in with first, then the object.
.SS Policy Management
A \fIpolicy\fR is the name of a policy, not a path.
It is looked for in the site of the scope.
Which policies there are, and what each of them does, is in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.TP
.B add_policy \fIscope\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Attach a policy to the scope.
.br
A policy has a list of object types it is for; one that does not name \fBscope\fR is refused.
So is a second policy of a type that allows only one per object.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBassign:policy\fR.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fIscope\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Remove a policy from the scope.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:policy\fR, and that one verbatim: a plain \fBremove\fR does not cover it.
Detaching a policy takes away what it enforces, so it must not be cheaper than attaching one.
.TP
.B list_policies \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIscope\fR
List the policies attached to the scope.
Use \fB\-\-return\-type\fR to select the attribute returned (\fBname\fR, \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR, \fBuuid\fR).
Use \fB\-\-policy\-types\fR to list only policies of the given types; these are policy types, not policy names.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:policies\fR.
.SS ACL Management
An ACL says what somebody may do with this scope.
The somebody is a role or a token, never a user.
.PP
What the three forms \fBacl\fR, \fB+acl\fR and \fB++acl\fR mean, and which ACLs exist, is under \fBACLs\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B add_acl \fIscope\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR][,\fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR]]
Give a role or a token one or more ACLs on this scope.
.br
\fIowner_type\fR is \fBrole\fR or \fBtoken\fR, and \fIowner\fR the path of that role or token.
An ACL may carry a value, for example \fBview:clients\fR, and several are separated by commas.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:acl\fR.
.TP
.B del_acl \fIscope\fR \fIacl\fR[,\fIacl\fR]
Take those ACLs away again.
.br
Here an \fIacl\fR is the whole entry, owner and all, the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: \fBrole:berlin/sso\-admins:view\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdelete:acl\fR.
.TP
.B list_acls \fIscope\fR
Show the ACLs that lie on this scope, i.e. who may do what with it.
Needs the ACL \fBview:acl\fR.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fIscope\fR
Let the scope inherit the default ACLs of the unit above it.
.RS
.PP
A scope does not, and that is on purpose: whoever may change a unit full of other objects should not get a say over who may hand out a scope by that alone.
.PP
Only default ACLs are inherited.
An ordinary ACL on the unit never is.
.PP
Moving the scope into another unit then takes away the default ACLs of the old unit and applies those of the new one.
While inheritance is off, a move leaves the ACLs of the scope as they are.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBenable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fIscope\fR
Stop inheriting them.
Only the ACLs on the scope itself count from then on.
Needs the ACL \fBdisable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.SS Configuration
A config parameter is looked for on the scope, then on the units above it, then on the site.
The first value found is the one that applies, and the realm holds none.
A site can turn that around for a parameter it names in
.B force_site_config_parameters\fR,
and then its own value wins (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
.PP
Which parameters a scope takes is in
.B CONFIG PARAMETERS
below.
.TP
.B config \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] \fIscope\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set a parameter on the scope.
.RS
.TP
.B \-d
Remove the parameter from the scope, or with a value only that one value from a list.
It is not set back to a default: what applies afterwards is whatever the unit or the site says.
.TP
.B \-a
Append the value to a parameter that holds a list, instead of replacing what is there.
.RE
.IP
Without a \fIvalue\fR the parameter is set to its default, which is not the same as leaving it unset: the value then stands on the scope and nothing above it is read any more.
.br
The ACL is per parameter, \fBset:config:\fIparameter\fR.
.TP
.B show_config \fIscope\fR \fR[\fIparameter\fR]
The config parameters set on the scope itself, or only the one named.
.br
Nothing is shown for a parameter that the scope gets from a unit or from the site, and nothing for one whose ACL \fBview:config:\fIparameter\fR you do not hold.
The scope ID and the members of the scope are not config parameters: \fBshow\fR and \fBexport\fR are where those are.
.TP
.B get_config \fIscope\fR \fIparameter\fR
What the parameter comes to for this scope, once the scope, its units and its site have been looked at.
So this answers what applies, where \fBshow_config\fR answers what was set here.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:config:\fIparameter\fR, the same one that opens it in \fBshow_config\fR.
.TP
.B description \fIscope\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set the description.
Without one OTPme asks and offers the current text for editing.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBedit:description\fR.
.TP
.B info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIscope\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set free-form info text.
If \fIinfo\fR is omitted, the current text is opened in the editor named by the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable.
.br
Without
.B \-\-language
the text is the administrator's own note about the object, which no user is ever shown.
With a language it is text for the users in that language.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBedit:info\fR.
.TP
.B dump_info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIscope\fR
Print the info text, of that language or the administrator's own.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:info\fR.
.SS Export and Cleanup
.TP
.B export \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassword\fR] \fIscope\fR
Write the whole object of the scope to stdout, as JSON.
.br
Without \fB\-\-password\fR it is written as it is, in clear.
With one, a key is derived from that password and the object is encrypted with it; the salt goes into the output, so the password is all you need to read it back.
.br
What a scope holds are references by UUID \(em its tokens, its roles, its clients, its groups \(em so an export says something only in the realm it came from.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBexport\fR.
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fIscope\fR
Remove references to objects that are gone.
.br
A token, a role, a group or a client that is deleted leaves its UUID behind in every scope that held it.
Nothing is handed out to it any more \(em the UUID resolves to nothing \(em but it stays in the object and \fBshow\fR prints it as a bare UUID.
This clears those out, together with orphan ACLs, policies and signatures.
.br
It asks once for everything it found; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:orphans\fR.
.SH HOW A SCOPE IS GRANTED
A login through the SSO portal grants a scope only if it is in all three of these:
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
what the application asked for, the \fIscope\fR parameter of its \fI/authorize\fR request,
.IP \(bu 2
what the token of the user may hand out: the scopes that hold the token, plus the scopes that hold one of its roles,
.IP \(bu 2
what the application may receive: the scopes that hold that client, roles not counted.
.RE
.PP
A disabled scope is in none of them.
.PP
Everything else is left out silently, in the order the application asked.
So an application that asks for \fBopenid profile email\fR and whose users have only \fBopenid\fR and \fBprofile\fR gets those two, and no error.
What it did get is in the answer of the token endpoint, as the \fIscope\fR field.
That is the shape of the mistake to look for: a user signs in, the application is happy, and it knows nothing about them because their token reaches no scope.
.PP
Nothing fills the user side by itself.
A role is put into a scope with
.B add_role
and stays there until it is taken out; the only ones that come with somebody in them are the three default scopes of a site, which hold its \fBSSO_USER\fR role.
.PP
A request that does not ask for \fBopenid\fR at all is a different matter: that one is refused with \fIinvalid_scope\fR, because OpenID Connect says every request has to carry it.
.PP
The claims of the granted scopes are handed out at \fI/userinfo\fR.
They are in the ID Token as well only where the client, its unit or its site sets
.B oidc_id_token_user_claims
(see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
.PP
What the user sees of all this is the consent screen of the portal, which lists the scopes of the request with the values they would hand over.
.SH THE SCOPE ID
The name of a scope is for the administrator, the scope ID is what goes over the wire.
.PP
A scope ID may be used by more than one scope object, and that is the point: every application that wants the \fBgroups\fR scope needs its own list of groups, so every one of them gets its own scope object with the scope ID \fBgroups\fR.
A client can be in one scope per scope ID \(em
.B add_client
refuses the second \(em so which object answers for which application is never in doubt.
.PP
.B add
takes the scope ID as its second argument, and without one it is the name of the scope.
.B scope_id
changes it afterwards.
.PP
The discovery document of the site lists the scope IDs of all its enabled scopes under \fIscopes_supported\fR, each one once.
Which application really gets which of them is still decided per scope object.
.SH THE GROUPS CLAIM
The \fBgroups\fR scope tells an application which groups the user is in, which is how applications like Nextcloud or Grafana decide what somebody may do inside them.
It is not a scope of the OIDC standard, but a widely used one.
.PP
What the application is told is the overlap of two lists: the groups of the user, and the groups on the scope.
A group the user is in and the scope does not name is not mentioned, and neither is one the scope names and the user is not in.
.PP
So the scope is the list of groups an application is allowed to know about.
An application for one department does not learn the group structure of the whole realm, and a group is never named to an application that was not meant to see it.
.PP
The list is empty to begin with, which means no group is ever named.
Adding the scope to an application is not enough; the groups have to be added as well.
.PP
The scope OTPme asks for this is the one whose \fBscope ID\fR is \fBgroups\fR and that holds this client.
.SH THE SCOPES A SITE BRINGS
Every site is created with seven scopes.
They cannot be renamed or deleted, only disabled.
.PP
Three of them \(em \fBopenid\fR, \fBprofile\fR and \fBemail\fR \(em are also written into the site parameter
.B oidc_default_scopes\fR,
which is the list a new OIDC client is given (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
Those three are given the \fBSSO_USER\fR role as well, so whoever may use the portal can hand them out, and an application created with the default scopes works without anybody being added to a scope.
.PP
The other four hold nobody.
A scope one of them is wanted from needs both sides named: the client with
.BR add_client ,
and the users with
.BR add_role .
.TP
.B openid
Carries no claim of its own.
Every request has to ask for it, and an application that has it not granted sees it missing from the answer.
.TP
.B profile
The name and the like: \fIname\fR, \fIgiven_name\fR, \fIfamily_name\fR, \fIpreferred_username\fR, and where the user has them \fImiddle_name\fR, \fInickname\fR, \fIwebsite\fR, \fIlocale\fR, \fIupdated_at\fR and \fIpicture\fR.
.br
They are read from the LDAP attributes of the user \(em \fBcn\fR, \fBgivenName\fR, \fBsn\fR, \fBinitials\fR, \fBdisplayName\fR, \fBlabeledURI\fR \(em and \fIlocale\fR only where the user set a language of their own.
\fIpicture\fR is a URL of the portal, and only where the user has a photo.
.TP
.B email
The \fIemail\fR claim, from the LDAP attribute \fBmail\fR, or from whatever the site names in
.B oidc_email_attribute\fR.
.TP
.B address
The \fIaddress\fR claim, built from \fBpostalAddress\fR or \fBstreet\fR, \fBl\fR, \fBst\fR, \fBpostalCode\fR and \fBc\fR.
.TP
.B phone
The \fIphone_number\fR claim, from the LDAP attribute \fBtelephoneNumber\fR.
.TP
.B offline_access
In OpenID Connect this is how an application says that it wants to go on working for the user while they are not there: it asks for a refresh token, with which it fetches new access tokens without the user signing in again.
.br
It carries no claim, and OTPme reads it nowhere.
Whether an application gets a refresh token is decided by its grant types alone \(em \fBrefresh_token\fR has to be one of them, see
.B add_oidc_grant_type
in
.BR otpme\-client (1)
\(em and nothing asks whether this scope was granted.
.br
So what it does today is appear in the \fIscope\fR field of the token answer, and on the consent screen, where the portal has a line for it (that screen is shown where
.B oidc_require_consent
is set, see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
Granting it to an application that has no \fBrefresh_token\fR grant type promises something it will not get; keeping it from one that has the grant type takes nothing away.
.TP
.B groups
The \fIgroups\fR claim, see \fBTHE GROUPS CLAIM\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option belongs to one command and is described with that command above.
.PP
Global options work with every command, see
.BR otpme (1).
.SH CONFIG PARAMETERS
A scope takes these:
.TP
.B changelog \fR(bool)
Whether the commands on this scope are written to its changelog.
.br
Default: true
.TP
.B allow_changelog_clearing \fR(bool)
Whether the changelog of this scope may be cleared, see
.B clear_changelog
above.
.br
Default: true
.PP
What each of them does, and how a parameter is resolved, is in
.BR otpme (7).
.PP
The parameters of the OIDC provider itself \(em \fBoidc_default_scopes\fR, \fBoidc_email_attribute\fR, \fBoidc_id_token_user_claims\fR and the rest \(em are set on the site, the unit or the client, not here.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Looking at what is there
.TP
.B otpme-scope show
All scopes of the site with their members and their scope IDs.
.TP
.B otpme-scope show profile
The \fBprofile\fR scope itself: who may hand it out and which applications get it.
.TP
.B otpme-scope list_clients email
The applications that are told the mail address.
.SS A new application
.TP
.B otpme-client add \-\-enable\-oidc wiki
Create the application.
It is given the default scopes of the site, which are \fBopenid\fR, \fBprofile\fR and \fBemail\fR.
Those three hold the \fBSSO_USER\fR role already, so nothing has to be added here for them.
.TP
.B otpme-accessgroup add_role wiki wiki-users
Let those users sign in to it.
Who gets in is the access group of the client \(em here one of the same name \(em and not the scope (see \fBotpme\-accessgroup\fR(1)).
.TP
.B otpme-scope add_client phone wiki
Let the application have the phone number as well.
.TP
.B otpme-scope add_role phone wiki-users
And let its users hand that one out.
Both lines are needed: the client side says which application may receive the scope, the role side who may give it away.
.SS Group memberships for one application
.TP
.B otpme-scope add wiki_groups groups
A scope of its own for the \fBwiki\fR, with the scope ID \fBgroups\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-scope add_client wiki_groups wiki
This one answers for the \fBwiki\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-scope add_group wiki_groups wiki-editors
.TP
.B otpme-scope add_group wiki_groups wiki-admins
The two groups the \fBwiki\fR may know about.
Every other group of the realm stays unmentioned.
.TP
.B otpme-scope add_role wiki_groups wiki-users
The users that hand the scope out.
.SS Keeping something to yourself
.TP
.B otpme-scope remove_client email mailinglist
The application no longer gets the mail address, whatever it asks for.
.TP
.B otpme-scope disable address
Nobody hands out postal addresses on this site any more, and the scope is gone from the discovery document.
.SS Handing the work over
.TP
.B otpme-scope add_acl wiki_groups role it\-support add:group,remove:group,view:groups
The service desk may change the group list of this one scope, and nothing else about it.
.TP
.B otpme-scope remove_orphans profile
Clear out the applications and tokens that were deleted since.
